-- ============================================
-- Script: 06_english_obras.sql
-- Descripción: Descripciones en inglés de las obras
-- ============================================

USE arte_asturiano;

-- JULIA ALCAYDE (ids 1–4)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The animals and objects composing this still life are rendered with great detail, while in the landscape the brushtrokes becomes looser and the composition more sketch-like. In the foreground the day\'s catch (hares and partridges) is piled up alongside the hunter\'s equipment: the satchel, the knife and the horn, in a clearly intended to lend immediacy and realism to the composition.' WHERE id = 1;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Representation of a still life in a brightly rural landscape. The wall of a rustic house in the background plays a prominent role in the composition, showing the textures of the different layers of whitewash, tiles and wood. In the foreground, a complete still life includes fruits, vegetables and game, alongside baskets of various wicker types and metal objects such as a Roman scale and a jug. It won the second medal at the National Fine Arts Exhibition of 1899.' WHERE id = 2;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Although landscape always plays a very important role in all her works, in this one, she has reduced the setting to a minimum, concentrating on the foreground elements of the composition. The still life, made up of peaches, grapes, plums and pomegranates, must have been painted around September, when the harvest brings those fruits together. The artist focuses on representing the different textures of the fruits through a realistic description. She submitted it along with three other works to the National Fine Arts Exhibition of 1912, winning a second medal once again.' WHERE id = 3;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Monochromatic drawing depicting several flowers in detail, identifiable visually as irises or lilies. The composition is vertical, showing a main stem from which several flowers branch in different stages of bloom, from closed buds to fully open flowers. The use of lead white together with graphite demonstrates great mastery of shading and texture to give volume to petals and leaves.' WHERE id = 4;

-- ENCARNA DÍAZ (ids 5–9)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Her earliest works are compositions of vibrant, intense yet harmonious colour, with broad patches of color in mixed technique, with titles that announce words or the beginnings of unfinished sentences.' WHERE id = 5;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Sculpture held great interest for the artist, although she did not turn to it until 2003. Her early work is notable for its bronze torsos, which exhibit an abstract tendency without losing figuration, with a greenish-black patina. The richness of planes in compositions, is striking, mantaining the sensuality of the forms and the variety of perspectives, at times evoking ancient Egyptian statuary.' WHERE id = 6;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Created as a tribute to the young footballer who died in a traffic accident in 2003. The artist imagined the player between veiled and present, in motion — a movement perceptible from any angle. It consists of a torso that is conceptually classicist at its core with a realistic touch, using the wet drapery technique. Without legs, it suggests the impossibility of real movement, the face tense and serene at the same time. The location, chosen by the family and the artist herself, enhances that sense of movement, as it appears that Armando is about to leap onto the field from the corner of the youngest section of the Real Oviedo supporters\' stand.' WHERE id = 7;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This is one of the works forming part of a series called "Light Boxes". In this series, she plays with a duality of perspective, with light and without it. They shift from flat painting to faint scenes with a surreal touch, where silhouettes of human figures, fish or birds can be seen, as in this case.' WHERE id = 8;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This year marks the beginning of a strong inspiration from Mexican art, where she completed her studies in Art History. This is one of 36 canvases made during this period. In it, the head and body of Balam, the jaguar, a totemic animal and deity in Mayan culture that represents the Sun in its journey through the underworld. The cool blues and greens with their counterpoints of yellow, red and ochre, create an abstract atmosphere that contrasts with the clear definition of the animal\'s eye and head profile.' WHERE id = 9;

-- TRINIDAD FERNÁNDEZ (ids 10–13)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This work exemplifies the artist\'s figurative period, characterized by a simplification and schematization of forms. Her work from this period has often been described as having an "naive or childlike touch". The painting captures an urban scene from the famous Montmartre district of Paris, known for being the epicenter of artistic life in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. The composition shows buildings with a pronounced perspective, a cloudy sky, and a solitary figure walking down the street, evoking an introspective atmosphere.' WHERE id = 10;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Asturian port landscape, belongs to her figurative period. The work depicts the port of Luarca, with houses terraced on a hill and boats in the water, capturing the essence of the Asturian coast.' WHERE id = 11;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Simplification of natural forms (mountains, valleys or meadows) using tonalities that evoke the atmosphere of Asturias (greens, greys, ochres), applied with a technique that prioritizes texture and the vibration of the material.' WHERE id = 12;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'In this work she explores the transition between reality and abstraction under dim lighting conditions. Unlike her luminous landscapes of Asturias or Luarca, in this piece, Fernández uses darkness to blur the boundaries of objects. Her palette is dominated by deep blues, blacks and grays, often interrupted by subtle points of light.' WHERE id = 13;

-- NICANOR PIÑOLE (ids 14–18)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Depicts the return at dusk from the pilgrimage celebrated in Perlora on the first Sunday of August, with the pilgrims ascending across the fields towards the village of Prendes. The serpentine line of figures is framed by a broad panorama of the coast of the municipality of Carreño, with the headland of San Antonio de Candás to the left. The scene unfolds under a diffuse light, in an overcast sunset that lends a certain drama to the whole.' WHERE id = 14;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The scene takes place at the end of summer in Carreño, in the steep orchard of Quinta de Chor estate, where the Prendes family spent their summers. In this large-format painting, a group of neighbors volunteer to complete the apple harvest. A large number of men, women and children, including Nicanor\'s nephews and nieces, work diligently to collect and transport the fruit to the cider press and begin the process of transforming it into natural cider. In the background, beneath a cloud-filled sky, a typical coastal house can be seen.' WHERE id = 15;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'It represents the destruction and victims caused during the siege of Gijón by the cruiser Almirante Cervera, which bombarded the city continuously, unleashing panic among the population. The "Cervera" symbolizes death advancing over chaos and destruction, in a disorder from which no one is spared. Industry, the arts, everything is destroyed by the advance of that cadaverous and phantasmagorical figure.' WHERE id = 16;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The strong bond between the artist and his mother is reflected in the large number of portraits he made of her throughout his life, the last of which he painted in 1951, shortly before her death. In this work, the painter demonstrates his extraordinary ability to capture the personality of his mother, a woman of strong and austere character. Seated in a solid armchair, Brígida is soberly dressed and maintains a serious, firm expression. Her figure stands out against a neutral background that focuses all attention on her hands and face, strongly illuminated by an indeterminate light, reminiscent of Velázquez tradition, enveloping the figure.' WHERE id = 17;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This is a sketch for a poster, a genre that experienced its golden age in the 1920s thanks to the increased advertising demand and institutional propaganda campaigns. In Gijón, the establishment of the Trade Fair in 1924, gave an impulse to activities related to promoting the city as a tourist destination. The protagonist of the composition is the red passenger bus of the "Chamico" company, which served the route between Tamón and Gijón. To the left, in the background, a car and another bus can be seen. The scene takes place in a peaceful rural setting with the Campa Torres headland and the now-vanished Aboño beach in the distance.' WHERE id = 18;

-- EVARISTO VALLE (ids 19–23)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The protagonists of the paint are a village couple appearing in the foreground carrying purple flowers symbolizing their enngagement. These two figures, rendered monumentally, show the influence of Zuloaga, although the wry, caricatured expressions on their faces corresponds to Valle\'s personal style. Behind them unfolds an Asturian landscape, illuminated by sunlight filtering through a cloud-filled sky, creating different areas of light and shadow.' WHERE id = 19;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This is one of his best-known works, modified throughout his career. The painting shows the traditional character of the Italian commedia dell\'arte, Pierrot, known as the sad clown suffering from unrequited love, dressed in his characteristic baggy, often white with large buttons, a cap and ruffled collar, and pale makeup. Valle returns to this archetype to reflect on the tension between the spiritual and the material, melancholy and humor, and the lyrical depth of life.' WHERE id = 20;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'A recurring theme in his work, this shows a scene of fishermen in a port. It portrays port life with groups of sailors chatting and working, capturing the atmosphere and local character of the region. The composition is dominated by a dark boat in the foreground and a background of fishermen\'s houses crowded onto a hillside, with vibrant touches of red sails or nets.' WHERE id = 21;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This is one of the many carnival scenes created from 1910 until the end of his life. It shows a series of figures in traditional costume (tunics, blouses, skirts, etc.), positioned on the outskirts of the city of Oviedo, recognizable by the cathedral in the background and the silhouette of Mount Naranco, which seems to be dressed up as the Sierra del Aramo behind it. Various cubic volumes form a cluster of houses that frame the main scene like a stage set or backdrop.' WHERE id = 22;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Ramona, Xuana and Cefera were the names Valle gave to The Three Witches. Two of them, seated on a wall next to the remains of a fence, whisper to each other; the third, standing, directs her gaze at the viewer. The composition is notable for its structure, defined by two diagonal lines that extend from the four corners and intersect in the center of the painting.' WHERE id = 23;

-- JOAQUÍN RUBIO CAMÍN (ids 24–28)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The apparently mundane aspects of the city of Gijón were elevated by the artist to something transcendent, such as the gasometer, a structure that became a symbolic icon of the city within his paintings and to which he constantly alluded throughout his career. The urban-themed paintings highlight and give meaning to what, at first glance, might appear uninteresting or even unpleasant to the viewer. Most often, this iconography of the gasometer is accompanied by other industrial elements such as chimneys and industrial buildings, as well as the large factory walls.' WHERE id = 24;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'His first steel sculptures date from 1961. It take two or three years, alternating this discipline with painting, before this new dedication became exclusive. From the very outset, an interest in playing with lines, volumes and the spatial development of his figures was evident, using the angle iron or dihedral as the form from which to articulate all his compositions. Cutting, bending and welding the material became from this moment the three technical gestures employed by the artist. This work, an abstract interpretation of a willow tree, is conceived as a simple concatenation of modules in which everything seems perfectly measured and proportioned, combining solidity and lightness in equal measure.' WHERE id = 25;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Sculpture configured from courses of brick that were subsequently carved as if they were a monolithic block of stone. Establishing connections with his smaller bronze sculpture, this Maternity is characterised as a closed structure of marked formal essentiality, evidencing a transcendental dimension within his sculptural career.' WHERE id = 26;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Located under the bridge over the Caudal river, near the "Gonzalín" Industrial Estate, although it has no name it is popularly known as "The Seagull" or "Bird" due to its forms suggesting the flight of the animal. It forms part of the artist\'s interventions along the riverside walk of Mieres, to which the design of the railing and the ornamental motifs of the river channel are also attributed.' WHERE id = 27;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Life-size bronze sculpture located on the riverside walk between Figaredo and Ujo. Made in collaboration with the company Bronces Artísticos S.L. This sculpture seeks to pay homage to all those women who worked as water carriers, a typical profession in the mining industry of the last century. These workers were responsible for bringing water to the miners as they emerged from the pit shafts after their working shifts.' WHERE id = 28;

-- ANTONIO SUÁREZ (ids 29–33)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The painting shows an industrial landscape, a recurring theme in his early career. The work has a figurative style with a focus on composition and structure, though with an expressive and impastoed brushwork. The scene is dominated by buildings with straight lines and simple geometric forms. A large industrial brick chimney and a large metal lattice structure stand out, rising above the surrounding houses.' WHERE id = 29;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This work belongs to his initial figurative period and shows an urban scene with a carousel as the central protagonist, a theme the artist represented on other occasions. The composition is dominated by the circular forms of the carousel, with white and red horses, and the structures of the trees and city buildings in the background. Two figures in red coats can be seen in the foreground to the left.' WHERE id = 30;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This painting, shown at the XXIX Venice Biennale, is a fine example of his early years of abstract work. Against a background dominated by cool tones applied through glazes of grey, brown and white, a cluster of patches floats, for whose definition the artist uses colours such as red, blue and gold. In contrast to the gravity and fierceness of the Abstract Expressionism of his El Paso colleagues, Suárez\'s work has always been characterised by a pursuit of elegance and chromatic refinement.' WHERE id = 31;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Suárez refrained from giving titles to his pictures, calling them generically "paintings". This procedure allows the viewer to interpret without prior conditioning what they are observing. This is the case here, although the inscription "El buey" (The Ox) on the reverse seems to indicate that it is inspired by Rembrandt\'s famous still life "The Slaughtered Ox", with which it shares compositional parallels.' WHERE id = 32;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'A clearly abstract composition, though with organic suggestions. Over a background of cool tones he arranges the pictorial matter from a central core, leaving the margins of the canvas free. The colour palette is limited: whites, greys and above all tawny and coppery tones applied with glazes that lend volume to the composition. In this case he introduces small wooden blocks which, arranged in a line, evoke fragments of mosaics.' WHERE id = 33;

-- JOSÉ MARÍA NAVASCUÉS (ids 34–38)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The first of a group of large-scale works that function as great artefacts with contrasting meanings. A playful object associated with rest acquires a different meaning when suspended from two ropes held by butcher\'s hooks. Furthermore, by covering it with a lid, he gives it the appearance of a coffin. This idea is reinforced by reliefs on the lid that suggest the presence of a figure trapped inside and struggling to escape.' WHERE id = 34;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Navascués showed a marked interest in the world and iconography of motor racing, creating sculptures that represent both the figure of a driver and of protective helmets. Moreover, in many of his drawings accidents and speed are the theme around which the representation revolves. In this work the central element is the figure of a driver hanging, by means of leather belts, from the curved bar of a racing car\'s chassis.' WHERE id = 35;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'A monumental work that stands out among the large sculptural artefacts made by the artist. The central element is the figure of the pilot and the two truncated pyramidal pieces corresponding to the hands, producing a hybrid between human form and machine.' WHERE id = 36;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Between 1974 and 1975 the artist created a group of pieces (guillotines, weapons and objects inspired by vampire iconography) whose meaning revolves around the idea of death, though stripped of all drama through the use of irony. Inside this box, Navascués arranged leather-lined compartments containing a stake and a hammer to kill vampires, confronting the tragic character of its meaning with the formal beauty and delicate execution of the whole piece.' WHERE id = 37;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'In this "anthropomorphic capsule" only the "wrapping" of the racing driver is shown: the helmet and the suit — something, therefore, fragmentary and decontextualised. For him, "the racing driver is, at one and the same time, driver and driven. Dominator and trapped. Open and enclosed. His external image is his own virtual image".' WHERE id = 38;

-- MARIANO MORÉ (ids 39–43)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'A work of social subject matter depicting the harsh reality of mining work in Asturias during the 1920s and 1930s. The artist captures the atmosphere of the mining sites and the social militancy of the era. Attention centres on the face of a young coal carrier, visibly exhausted, who can barely hold his gaze. A large sack on his head occupies most of the foreground. The mountainous background fades into the composition, while to the right the silhouette of a factory installation is suggested.' WHERE id = 39;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The scene shows four miners walking towards the viewer, with fatigue reflected in their faces and postures. They wear dark work clothes and some carry closed umbrellas, suggesting a rainy or overcast day. The background shows an industrial and semi-urban landscape with houses, a smoking chimney and a mountain.' WHERE id = 40;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'Propaganda poster in favour of children\'s rights, from the Institute of Child Health of Gijón. Illustrated sheet showing a baby behind a lectern and, in the background, children with placards demanding their rights.' WHERE id = 41;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'After the Spanish Civil War, the vision of Asturian reality became much more softened and figuration tended towards geometrisation. The artist endows the work with a contained drama by placing those four women in the foreground, with their backs to the viewer, directing their gaze towards a sea of threatening waves that bodes no good news for any fishing boats that might be at sea.' WHERE id = 42;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'A softened vision of Asturian reality, tending towards Costumbrism. The composition is monumental and focuses on the visual narrative of the women, who advance in an ascending procession along a steep path. The figures are arranged in the foreground, guiding the viewer\'s eye through the scene. In the background the traditional whitewashed fishermen\'s houses can be seen, with laundry hanging from the balconies, and a detailed mountainous and coastal landscape.' WHERE id = 43;

-- PELAYO ORTEGA (ids 44–48)

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The centre is occupied by a tangle of bright yellow strokes that resemble a cage, scaffolding or a chaotic structure. A hanging lamp illuminates the central structure and surrounding objects intensely against a black background, creating a strong contrast and a dramatic, intimate atmosphere typical of a workshop or nocturnal scene. At the bottom two chairs can be seen, one with a yellow seat and a light blue back, and another with a red seat and a blue back. To the right, the schematic silhouette of a human figure seen from behind introduces a narrative element. The work evokes solitude, transit and reflection, recurring themes in Ortega\'s output, in which the viewer is invited to become the protagonist of the scene.' WHERE id = 44;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'This is a lithograph in which Pelayo Ortega deploys his characteristic style, mixing figurative and abstract elements with an aesthetic reminiscent of comics and Pop Art. The work presents a composition of flat planes of colour and thick black lines that delimit the forms. The colour is vibrant, with areas of yellow, green, red and grey that contrast strongly with one another.' WHERE id = 45;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The work generates a stage-like vision of New York, treated almost like an illusion or a great theatrical set, using meticulous and subtle workmanship. It combines geometric and material abstraction in planes of colour (predominantly greys, turquoises and oranges) with a clear figurative element: a small human silhouette seen from behind. The use of artificial light stands out, such as the street lamp at the centre, which illuminates the scene and contrasts with the darker tones and the texture of the canvas.' WHERE id = 46;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The title is taken from the short story of the same name by the writer Jorge Luis Borges. The work is dominated by a central patch of intense white light from which fine lines and an effect of cosmic explosion or implosion emanate. The composition centres on this point of visual convergence. The title makes a direct reference to Borges\'s concept of the "Aleph": a point at which all the places in the world, seen from every angle, can be found without confusion. The painting attempts to visually represent this simultaneous and boundless vision of the universe.' WHERE id = 47;

UPDATE obras SET descripcion_en = 'The composition is dominated by geometric planes of colour and thick black lines that create a lattice-like structure, reminiscent of urban architecture. It presents a palette of cool colours, predominantly blues, turquoises and greys, with a bright yellow plane that stands out at the centre. A small human silhouette seen from behind, located within the lit central plane, introduces a narrative and symbolic element. This figure, often a mysterious flâneur or passer-by, contrasts with the geometric coldness of the surroundings, evoking sensations of solitude, reflection and the passage of time.' WHERE id = 48;

SELECT 'Descripciones en inglés de las obras actualizadas correctamente.' AS Mensaje;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS obras_con_descripcion_en FROM obras WHERE descripcion_en IS NOT NULL;
